<span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.7.5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:51.2pt;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFCC;">
<span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.7.5.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.7.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Yes‡</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.7.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.7.6.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:51.2pt;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFCC;">
<span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.7.6.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.7.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">High</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.7.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.7.7.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:37.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:#FFFFCC;">
<span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.7.7.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S2.T1.8.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1.7.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PO</span></span>
</span></span></span>
</span>
</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></span>
</span></span></span><span id="S2.T1.8.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">
</span></p>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Continual Learning Mechanisms for Agents.</span> Continual learning (CL)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite> enables intelligent agents to incrementally adapt to new tasks while minimizing catastrophic forgetting<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. Recent work on CL for LLM-based agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> emphasizes memory management and online learning, often drawing from cognitive principles. <span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Memory management</span> is critical for retaining knowledge across tasks. Inspired by human episodic memory, experience replay methods store past interactions for periodic retraining <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. For example, CLIN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> continually updates memory with causal abstractions for dynamic adaptation, while Generative Agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> retrieve experiences based on recency and relevance, forming structured reflections to support reasoning. MemGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> enables LLMs to manage working and long-term memory in task sequences. However, these approaches typically target static or narrow task domains, limiting their scalability in more diverse, interactive environments. <span id="S2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Online learning</span> supports real-time adaptation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>]</cite>. Early works such as Reflexion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>, AdaPlanner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>, and ReAct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite> enable agents to adjust planning based on feedback. Reflexion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> allows agents to reflect on failures, and RAP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> uses retrieved experiences for decision-making in task-switching. Voyager <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite> lets agents store verified programs as reusable skills in dynamic environments. Despite progress, these methods often underperform in long-horizon tasks with delayed feedback and generally lack mechanisms for autonomous policy evolution. <span id="S2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Limitations remain</span>: Most memory and online learning frameworks lack support for real-time, autonomous policy evolution in truly interactive, dynamic environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. AutoManual <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> introduces dynamic rules for adaptation in ALFWorld and MiniWoB++, while Agent-Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>]</cite> refines strategies in card games via policy-level reflection. However, both are limited to specific domains and lack generalizability. Furthermore, existing methods seldom allow for parameter-free, real-time adaptation post-deployment, restricting alignment with human-like continual learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>. <span id="S2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Our approach</span> addresses these gaps by proposing a human-inspired online learning workflow (see section <a href="#S4" title="4 Methods ‣ EvoEmpirBench: Dynamic Spatial Reasoning with Agent-ExpVer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) that enables agents to abstract and update knowledge through continuous interaction—supporting dynamic, real-time, and parameter-free continual adaptation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Games in EvoEmpirBench</h2>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12718v1/agent_intro_new.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:636/207;" width="636" height="207" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of EvoEmpirBench: Locally observable maze navigation (left) and match-2 elimination (right), illustrating dynamic and interactive challenges for language agents.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">EvoEmpirBench (EEB) consists of two dynamic, interactive game environments designed to comprehensively evaluate LLM reasoning in complex, partially observable settings: a locally observable maze navigation task and a match-2 elimination game (Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Games in EvoEmpirBench ‣ EvoEmpirBench: Dynamic Spatial Reasoning with Agent-ExpVer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Both games simulate real-world uncertainty and agent-environment dynamics, demanding skills in spatial analysis, language understanding, and adaptive planning. Each game provides 3 progressively difficult levels (Easy, Medium, Hard) for both training and testing, totaling 120 diverse task episodes. EEB supports both human and agent operation, enabling flexible evaluation under different paradigms. We now outline the core rules, difficulty scaling, and evaluation metrics.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Game Rules</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Maze Navigation.</span> In this 9<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>9 grid world, agents act under local observability—exploring to reveal new tiles while aiming to reach a goal with high score. Main objectives include collecting five gold coins and exploring unknown areas efficiently. As difficulty increases, agents encounter new elements: Easy levels feature a simple map; Medium introduces two moving monsters that threaten agent health; Hard adds four interactive items—Pickaxe (breaks obstacles), Iron Sword (unlimited monster combat), Magnet (area coin collection), and Key (needed for exit)—along with the existing challenges. Many agent actions, such as obtaining weapons or destroying obstacles, alter the environment dynamically, requiring adaptive, multi-step reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Match-2 Elimination.</span> Agents operate on an 8<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>8 board, eliminating connected blocks of identical color (A–D) by matching two or more, with exponentially increasing rewards for larger matches. The primary goal is to meet per-color elimination targets within a limited number of moves, striving to maximize overall score and efficiency. Following each elimination, remaining blocks settle downward and new blocks spawn at the top. Agents may use step-limited props (row/col clear, bomb, hammer) at point costs to optimize gameplay. Difficulty levels vary step constraints and target requirements.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Difficulty Settings</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As summarized in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.2 Difficulty Settings ‣ 3 Games in EvoEmpirBench ‣ EvoEmpirBench: Dynamic Spatial Reasoning with Agent-ExpVer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, EEB increases challenge by scaling environment complexity and resource constraints. In maze navigation, more complex maps, adversaries, and items are layered at higher levels to push planning and adaptation. In match-2, shrinking move allowances and rising elimination targets require increasingly efficient strategies.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span><span id="S3.T2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Difficulty settings in EvoEmpirBench.</span> Maze navigation raises environmental complexity; match-2 tightens step budgets and goals.</figcaption>
