<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">How much do EEB’s constraints affect model performance?</span> To evaluate, we conduct ablation studies on both games. As reported in Table <a href="#S5.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 5.1 Ablation Studies ‣ 5 Experiment ‣ EvoEmpirBench: Dynamic Spatial Reasoning with Agent-ExpVer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, in the Maze Game, removing exploration via global visibility (Full-Vision) leads to a notable performance boost: GPT-4.1’s success rate rises from 73.33% (EEB) to 93.33%, and its average score increases from 2562.33 to 3412.22. Qwen2.5 shows a similar trend, with success rate improving from 42.22% to 57.78%. Both models also require fewer steps under Full-Vision, indicating reduced reasoning complexity.In the Match-2 Game, prohibiting tool use (NoProps) slightly improves scores for both models, but the gains are less pronounced than in Maze. For example, GPT-4.1’s success rate increases from 40.00% (EEB) to 43.33%, and Qwen2.5’s score rises from 203.07 to 411.62.These results demonstrate that EEB’s design—through partial observability and tool-based reasoning—significantly increases task difficulty and reasoning demands, as evidenced by consistent performance drops compared to control variants.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span><span id="S5.T7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Performance Comparison on Maze and Match<span id="S5.T7.3.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>2 Games.</span> Explanation of Metrics and Abbreviations:
EEB = Embodied Environment Baseline.
Full-Vision = Model has full visual information.
NoProps = No propositional information provided.</figcaption>
