<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix A: ExpVer-Agent Mechanism for Generation-Oriented Truth Formation</h2>

<div id="Ax1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix details the ExpVer-Agent mechanism, an online learning approach based on “subjective experience–verification–truth induction,” designed to enhance agent performance in maze navigation tasks. The core procedure consists of the following steps:</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generation of Subjective Experience</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In each level, the agent first acts as an “explorer,” completing a full game episode. During this process, the system automatically records the agent’s reasoning (i.e., decision-making analysis at each step), actions taken, and key performance indicators, including but not limited to:</p>
<ul id="Ax1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Ax1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Total score</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ax1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Success or failure</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ax1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Remaining lives</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ax1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Number of coins collected</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ax1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Exploration rate</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ax1.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax1.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Number of monsters defeated (Level 3 only)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ax1.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax1.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Number of obstacles destroyed (Level 3 only)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ax1.I1.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax1.I1.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.I1.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Total steps taken</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="Ax1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">For example, after the agent’s initial attempt on the third map of Level 2, the system records both the reasoning process and performance metrics. The reflection module then summarizes the strengths and weaknesses of this attempt, forming the “subjective memory” for that level.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Ax1.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12718v1/purse_profit_experience.png" id="Ax1.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:720/405;" width="720" height="405" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span> <span id="Ax1.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Subjective experience guided by adventure</span> </figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="Ax1.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12718v1/safe_experience.png" id="Ax1.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:720/405;" width="720" height="405" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span> <span id="Ax1.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Subjective experience of survival orientation</span> </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Ax1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Verification of Subjective Experience</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The agent then reattempts the same level, this time guided by the previously summarized strengths and weaknesses. The system again records the performance metrics. If there is a significant improvement (e.g., higher score, successful completion), the subjective memory is considered effective and promoted to “truth knowledge,” which is added to a global knowledge base for future reference.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Induction and Consolidation of Truths</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">As training progresses, the truth knowledge base accumulates experiences from different levels and episodes. To prevent redundancy, the system periodically consolidates the knowledge base by:</p>
<ul id="Ax1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Ax1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Removing duplicate entries</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ax1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Merging similar experiences with different expressions into more representative, generalized knowledge points</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ax1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Retaining key information from original entries to preserve detail</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="Ax1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">For instance, two similar entries regarding monster avoidance may be merged into a single, more comprehensive guideline.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The evolution of generation-oriented truths in the agent’s learning process is clearly reflected in Figure <a href="#Ax1.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Appendix A: ExpVer-Agent Mechanism for Generation-Oriented Truth Formation ‣ EvoEmpirBench: Dynamic Spatial Reasoning with Agent-ExpVer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and Figure <a href="#Ax1.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix A: ExpVer-Agent Mechanism for Generation-Oriented Truth Formation ‣ EvoEmpirBench: Dynamic Spatial Reasoning with Agent-ExpVer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In the early stage (see Figure <a href="#Ax1.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Appendix A: ExpVer-Agent Mechanism for Generation-Oriented Truth Formation ‣ EvoEmpirBench: Dynamic Spatial Reasoning with Agent-ExpVer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), the agent’s subjective experience was characterized by a strong tendency toward risk and reward. For instance, the agent frequently prioritized “Active Exploration” and “Risk-Taking,” aggressively venturing into unvisited or potentially dangerous areas in pursuit of higher scores. This is further evidenced by the “Quick Objective Pursuit,” where the agent attempted to collect coins and reach the exit as soon as possible. However, such strategies often led to “Poor Survival Awareness,” with the agent ignoring threats and losing all lives early in the episode. The tendency to “Overgeneralize” from occasional successes—assuming that bold exploration always yields high rewards—resulted in inconsistent performance and frequent failures, as the agent did not sufficiently adapt its strategy after negative outcomes.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">After several rounds of iterative learning and reflection, the agent’s approach shifted significantly, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#Ax1.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix A: ExpVer-Agent Mechanism for Generation-Oriented Truth Formation ‣ EvoEmpirBench: Dynamic Spatial Reasoning with Agent-ExpVer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. At this stage, the agent’s subjective experience emphasized “Enhanced Threat Management” and “Cautious Exploration.” The agent learned to prioritize survival by actively avoiding monsters and hazards, and adopted a more stepwise, risk-averse approach to map exploration. Notably, the agent demonstrated “Adaptive Reasoning,” adjusting its strategy based on previous failures—such as learning to avoid repeating risky moves that previously led to loss of lives. This resulted in “Efficient Resource Use,” with the agent achieving higher scores and collecting more coins while maintaining safety. Although this cautious approach sometimes led to “Slower Progress” and occasional missed opportunities for rapid point accumulation, it marked a clear improvement in the agent’s ability to balance exploration with survival, leading to more consistent and successful task completion.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, the transition from the early, risk-seeking strategy to a more survival-oriented approach demonstrates the effectiveness of the ExpVer-Agent mechanism in enabling the agent to refine its decision-making process, ultimately achieving a better balance between exploration and safety in complex environments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix B: Prompts Used in EEB Games</h2>

<div id="Ax2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix provides the detailed prompt designs used in the two GAME modules of the EEB system: (1) prompts for agent-environment interaction, and (2) prompts for the ExpVer (Experience Verification) module. These prompts are critical for guiding the large language model (LLM) to perform effective reasoning, decision-making, and self-reflection in the maze navigation tasks.</p>
</div>
<section id="Ax2.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">B.1 Agent-Environment Interaction Prompts</h3>

<div id="Ax2.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax2.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For both GAME 1 and GAME 2, the agent interacts with the maze environment through carefully constructed prompts. The prompts are designed to provide the LLM with comprehensive information about the current game state, available actions, and key objectives, ensuring that the agent can make informed and context-aware decisions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax2.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax2.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax2.SSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example Prompt for Maze Navigation Game:</span></p><pre id="Ax2.SSx1.p2.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
You are an intelligent agent solving a maze problem.
Your task is to navigate through the maze efficiently while collecting rewards and avoiding
dangers.

Core Game Elements:
- A: Your current position
- G: Goal (always visible)
- C: Coin (+500 points)
- #: Wall (costs life if hit)
- ?: Unexplored area
- .: Empty space

Your Priorities (in order):
1. Stay alive (avoid walls/monsters)
2. Reach the goal
3. Collect coins when safe
4. Explore efficiently
5. Minimize steps

You will receive the current game state and must choose an action (0-11) based on careful
analysis of the situation.
Always explain your reasoning before making a decision.

Level 1 Characteristics:
- 9x9 grid size
- No monsters
- 5 coins to collect
- Focus on basic navigation and coin collection

Level 2 Characteristics:
- 9x9 grid size
- Contains monsters (M) that move randomly
- 5 coins to collect
- Requires careful planning to avoid monsters

Level 3 Characteristics:
- 9x9 grid size
- Contains monsters and special items
- Items available:
  * T: Shovel (break walls, 3 uses)
  * W: Sword (defeat monsters)
  * N: Magnet (attract nearby coins)
  * K: Key (required for goal)
- Most complex navigation

Training Knowledge (use these insights to make better decisions):
1. [knowledge 1]
2. [knowledge 2]
... (Each item from truth_knowledge, one per line)

Current Game State:
[Map string generated by MapProcessor.format_map_for_agent(), e.g.]:
   0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8
  ---------------------
 0 | . . . ? ? ? ? ? ?
 1 | . A . ? ? ? ? ? ?
 2 | . . . ? ? ? ? ? ?
 3 | . . . ? ? ? ? ? ?
 4 | . . . ? ? ? ? ? ?
 5 | . . . ? ? ? ? ? ?
 6 | . . . ? ? ? ? ? ?
 7 | . . . ? ? ? ? ? ?
 8 | . . . ? ? ? ? ? ?

Current position (row,col): (1,1)

[If full vision:]
NOTE: You have full vision of the entire map. You can see all obstacles, coins, monsters,
and items without the need to explore.

Game Status:
- Score: [current score]
- Lives: [current lives]
- Current Position (row,col): ([row],[col])

Movement System:
- Actions 0-2: Move UP (row-1) [1/2/3 steps]
- Actions 3-5: Move DOWN (row+1) [1/2/3 steps]
- Actions 6-8: Move LEFT (col-1) [1/2/3 steps]
- Actions 9-11: Move RIGHT (col+1) [1/2/3 steps]

Available Actions:
- 0: Move UP 1 step
- 1: Move UP 2 steps
- 2: Move UP 3 steps
- 3: Move DOWN 1 step
- 4: Move DOWN 2 steps
- 5: Move DOWN 3 steps
- 6: Move LEFT 1 step
- 7: Move LEFT 2 steps
- 8: Move LEFT 3 steps
- 9: Move RIGHT 1 step
- 10: Move RIGHT 2 steps
- 11: Move RIGHT 3 steps

[If Level 3, add item status:]
Current items status:
- Shovel: Equipped (Uses remaining: [number]) / Not equipped
- Sword: Equipped / Not equipped
- Magnet: Equipped / Not equipped
- Key: Collected / Not collected (required to finish)

Scoring System:
- New cell explored: +10 points
- Coin collected: +500 points
- Step taken: -50 points
- Life lost: -1000 points
- Goal reached: +2000 points

Please analyze the current situation and choose your next action:
1. Analyze visible area and potential risks
2. Consider exploration value and rewards
3. Choose action number (0-11)

[If Level 2 or 3:]
- You cannot touch monsters (M), or you’ll lose a life and return to start

[If Level 3:]
Special rules for Level 3:
- You must collect the key (K) before you can enter the goal
- With a shovel (T), you can break through walls without losing lives (3 uses)
- With a sword (W), you can defeat monsters without losing lives
- With a magnet (N), you can collect coins in a 5x5 area around you

The ultimate goal is to explore the map, collect coins, reach the goal, while maintaining a
high score

Response format:
1. First analyze the current situation, including explored areas, coin positions, and
potential risks
2. Consider possible movement options and their consequences, especially focusing on
exploration value
3. Finally, provide your choice using the format "Action: X" where X is a number
between 0-
11

For example:
After analysis, you should write "Action: 9" to indicate moving right by 1 step
</pre>
</div>
<div id="Ax2.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax2.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax2.SSx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example Prompt for Match-2 Game:</span></p><pre id="Ax2.SSx1.p3.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
system_prompt = """
You are an AI assistant for an 8x8 match game (gridSize = 8). The board is an
8x8 grid with colors A, B, C, D, or null (empty). Rules:
- Eliminate \geq2 connected same-color tiles (horizontal/vertical), score = tiles *
5 + 3 * max(0, tiles - 2).
- Props (each usable once): row (clear row, 32 points), col (clear column, 32
points), bomb (clear 3x3 area, 12 points), hammer (clear 1 tile, 4 points).
- Each action costs 1 step. Goal: Clear the level by meeting color elimination
targets (A, B, C, D) within limited steps while maximizing score and minimizing
steps used.
- Primary objective: Ensure level completion by achieving all color targets.
- Secondary objectives: Maximize total score by prioritizing larger tile
eliminations and efficient prop usage; minimize steps to preserve remaining
steps.
- After elimination, new random tiles (A, B, C, D) fall from the top to fill
empty spaces.
- Input: Board (8x8, A/B/C/D/null), score, steps remaining, inventory
(row/col/bomb/hammer), color targets, current color counts.
- Output: Best action in JSON: {"action": {"type":
"eliminate"|"row"|"col"|"bomb"|"hammer", "pos": [i,j] (for
eliminate/bomb/hammer, 0\leqi,j&lt;8), "index": k (for row/col, 0\leqk&lt;8)}}.
- If no valid action, return {"action": null}.
"""
</pre>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax2.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">B.2 ExpVer Module Prompts</h3>

<div id="Ax2.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax2.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The ExpVer (Experience Verification) module is responsible for guiding the agent in self-reflection, hypothesis testing, and truth induction. The prompts for this module are designed to elicit a structured analysis of the agent’s previous actions, outcomes, and reasoning, enabling the system to summarize strengths, weaknesses, and actionable insights.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax2.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax2.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax2.SSx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example ExpVer Prompt:</span></p><pre id="Ax2.SSx2.p2.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
# Game Session Analysis

Please analyze the following game session and provide insights about the agent’s
performance.

## Game Metrics:
- [key1]: [value1]
- [key2]: [value2]
...

## Session Highlights:

Step 1 (Beginning):
Agent’s reasoning: [first completion text]...

Step 2 (Beginning):
Agent’s reasoning: [second completion text]...

...

Step N (End):
Agent’s reasoning: [last completion text]...

## Analysis Tasks:

1. List the strengths demonstrated in this session. Provide as many as you can
identify.

2. List the weaknesses or areas for improvement from this session. Provide as
many as you can identify.

Please format your response as follows:

Strengths:
- [Strength 1]
- [Strength 2]
- [Strength 3]
...

Weaknesses:
- [Weakness 1]
- [Weakness 2]
- [Weakness 3]
...
</pre>
</div>
<div id="Ax2.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para"><pre id="Ax2.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
# Game Truth Knowledge Organization Task

Please review and organize the following game truth knowledge entries. This is a
progressive knowledge organization process to identify and remove duplicates
while considering merging highly similar entries.

## Current Knowledge Entries:
1. [Knowledge 1] (Source: ...)
2. [Knowledge 2] (Source: ...)
...

## Organization Requirements:
1. Identify and remove completely duplicate knowledge entries
2. For knowledge entries that are highly similar in meaning but different in
expression, merge them into a more comprehensive entry
3. When merging, preserve the specificity and core meaning of the original
knowledge, don’t lose key details
4. Merged entries should be concise but not at the expense of important
information
5. If two knowledge points only have minimal similarities, keep them as separate
entries
6. Knowledge entries without clear similarities should remain unchanged

## Please return the organized knowledge base in the following format:
[Organized knowledge entry 1]
[Organized knowledge entry 2] ...

Note: This is a progressive knowledge organization process, you do not need to
force a reduction in the number of entries. Only merge or remove entries when
there is genuine high similarity or duplication.
</pre>
</div>
<div id="Ax2.SSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax2.SSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These prompt templates ensure that the LLM agent can both interact effectively with the environment and perform meaningful self-assessment, supporting the iterative learning and truth induction process central to the EEB framework.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Ax3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix C. Example Agent Reasoning Trace (Maze Game, Level 1, GPT-4.1)</h2>

<div id="Ax3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix demonstrates the actual reasoning process of the LLM agent (GPT-4.1) in the first two steps of Level 1 in GAME1. Each step includes the system prompt, the game state, and the model’s full reasoning and decision.</p>
</div>
<section id="Ax3.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Step 0</h3>

<div id="Ax3.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax3.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax3.SSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">System Prompt:</span></p><pre id="Ax3.SSx1.p1.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
You are an intelligent agent solving a maze problem. Your task is to navigate through the
maze efficiently while collecting rewards and avoiding dangers.

Core Game Elements:
- A: Your current position
- G: Goal (always visible)
- C: Coin (+500 points)
- #: Wall (costs life if hit)
- ?: Unexplored area
- .: Empty space

Your Priorities (in order):
1. Stay alive (avoid walls/monsters)
2. Reach the goal
3. Collect coins when safe
4. Explore efficiently
5. Minimize steps

You will receive the current game state and must choose an action (0-11) based on careful
analysis of the situation.
Always explain your reasoning before making a decision.
Level 1 Characteristics:
- 7x7 grid size
- No monsters
- 5 coins to collect
- Focus on basic navigation and coin collection
</pre>
</div>
<div id="Ax3.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax3.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax3.SSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Game State (partial):</span></p><pre id="Ax3.SSx1.p2.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
Current Game State:
Game Coordinate System (row,col):
- Rows: 0 (top) to N (bottom)
- Cols: 0 (left) to N (right)

   0  1  2  3  4  5  6  7  8
  ----------------------------
 0 | ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ?
 1 | ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ?
 2 | ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ?
 3 | ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ?
 4 | ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ?
 5 | ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ?
 6 | ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ?
 7 | . . ? ? ? ? ? ? ?
 8 | A . ? ? ? ? ? ? ?

Current position (row,col): (8,0)
</pre>
</div>
<div id="Ax3.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax3.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax3.SSx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Reasoning and Decision:</span></p><pre id="Ax3.SSx1.p3.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
### 1. Situation Analysis

- **Visible Area:**
  The only explored cells are:
  - (8,0): Agent’s current position (A)
  - (7,0): Explored (.)
  - (7,1): Explored (.)
  - (8,1): Explored (.)

  All other cells are unexplored (?).

- **Coins:**
  No coins are currently visible.

- **Obstacles/Monsters:**
  None are visible.

- **Risks:**
  No immediate risks. All adjacent cells that are explored are safe (empty).

### 2. Movement Options &amp; Consequences

- **Possible Moves:**
  From (8,0), the agent can move:
  - UP to (7,0): Already explored, no exploration points, but safe.
  - RIGHT to (8,1): Already explored, no exploration points, but safe.
  - Any other direction (LEFT or DOWN) would go out of bounds (since (8,0) is at the
  bottom-left corner), so those are not valid.

- **Exploration Value:**
  To maximize exploration (and thus score), the agent should move into unexplored
  territory.
  The only way to do this is to move UP or RIGHT first (to a safe, explored cell), then
  from there, move into unexplored areas.

- **Step Minimization:**
  Since both (7,0) and (8,1) are equally valid, but both are already explored, the agent
  will not gain exploration points for the next move. However, moving to (8,1) (RIGHT) may
  open up more unexplored territory to the right, while moving UP to (7,0) may open up
  more unexplored territory above.

- **Preference:**
  Both options are equally safe and equally efficient at this stage. Either is acceptable,
  but moving RIGHT (Action 9) may be slightly preferable, as it keeps the agent on the
  bottom row, potentially allowing for more lateral exploration before moving up.

### 3. Decision

**Action: 9** (Move RIGHT 1 step to (8,1))

**Reasoning:**
- Safe move (no risk)
- Prepares for further exploration into the right side of the map
- Keeps options open for exploring both up and right in future moves

---

**Action: 9**
</pre>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax3.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Step 1</h3>

<div id="Ax3.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax3.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax3.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">System Prompt:</span></p><pre id="Ax3.SSx2.p1.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
You are an intelligent agent solving a maze problem. Your task is to navigate through the
maze efficiently while collecting rewards and avoiding dangers.

Core Game Elements:
- A: Your current position
- G: Goal (always visible)
- C: Coin (+500 points)
- #: Wall (costs life if hit)
- ?: Unexplored area
- .: Empty space

Your Priorities (in order):
1. Stay alive (avoid walls/monsters)
2. Reach the goal
3. Collect coins when safe
4. Explore efficiently
5. Minimize steps

You will receive the current game state and must choose an action (0-11) based on careful
analysis of the situation.
Always explain your reasoning before making a decision.
Level 1 Characteristics:
- 7x7 grid size
- No monsters
- 5 coins to collect
- Focus on basic navigation and coin collection
</pre>
</div>
<div id="Ax3.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax3.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax3.SSx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Game State (partial):</span></p><pre id="Ax3.SSx2.p2.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
Current Game State:
Game Coordinate System (row,col):
- Rows: 0 (top) to N (bottom)
- Cols: 0 (left) to N (right)

   0  1  2  3  4  5  6  7  8
  ----------------------------
 0 | ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ?
 1 | ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ?
 2 | ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ?
 3 | ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ?
 4 | ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ?
 5 | ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ?
 6 | ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ?
 7 | . . # ? ? ? ? ? ?
 8 | ? A C ? ? ? ? ? ?

Current position (row,col): (8,1)
</pre>
</div>
<div id="Ax3.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax3.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax3.SSx2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Reasoning and Decision:</span></p><pre id="Ax3.SSx2.p3.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
### 1. Situation Analysis

- **Current Position:** (8,1)
- **Visible Map:** Only the bottom two rows (rows 7 and 8) are partially visible.
    - (8,2): Coin (C)
    - (7,2): Wall (#)
    - (7,0), (7,1): Empty (.)
    - All other cells: Unexplored (?)
- **Obstacles:** Wall at (7,2)
- **Coins:** Only one visible coin at (8,2), directly to the right of the agent.
- **No monsters** are present.
- **Goal (G):** Not yet visible.

**Risks:**
- The only immediate risk is the wall at (7,2). Moving up from (8,2) would hit the wall,
but moving right from (8,1) to (8,2) is safe.

### 2. Movement Options and Consequences

#### Immediate Options:
- **Move RIGHT (Action 9):** Move to (8,2), collect the coin (+500), explore a new cell
(+10), cost 1 step (-50). Net gain: +460 points. No risk.
- **Move UP (Action 0):** Move to (7,1), explore a new cell (+10), cost 1 step (-50). No
immediate reward, but opens up exploration vertically.
- **Move LEFT (Action 6):** Move to (8,0), unexplored cell, similar exploration value as
moving up.
- **Move UP 2/3, LEFT 2/3, RIGHT 2/3:** All would move into unexplored territory, but
since we know there is a coin directly to the right, the most efficient and rewarding move
is to collect it first.

#### Strategic Considerations:
- **Priority 1:** Stay alive (no risk in moving right).
- **Priority 2:** Reach the goal (not visible yet, so explore efficiently).
- **Priority 3:** Collect coins (coin is immediately available).
- **Priority 4:** Explore efficiently (moving right will also reveal more of row 8 and
possibly row 7).
- **Priority 5:** Minimize steps (moving right is a single step and optimal for reward).

### 3. Decision

The best move is to collect the coin at (8,2) by moving right 1 step. This is safe,
maximizes immediate reward, and continues exploration.

**Action: 9**
</pre>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Ax4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix D. Example Agent Reasoning Trace (Match-2 Game, esay, GPT-4.1)</h2>

<div id="Ax4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix demonstrates the actual reasoning process of the LLM agent (GPT-4.1) in the
first several steps of Level 1 in Match-2 Game (easy). Each step includes the board state,
action, and the model’s full reasoning and decision.</p>
</div>
<section id="Ax4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Step 1</h3>

<div id="Ax4.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax4.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax4.SSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Board State (partial):</span></p><pre id="Ax4.SSx1.p1.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
C D D A B B C B
C C C B A A B C
A B B C B D B D
C C B A B B A B
B D C B A D A B
A B B D A C A A
D B A C C D B D
D B A A A C B D
Score: 0, Steps remaining: 15
Inventory: row=0, col=1, bomb=2, hammer=1
Color targets: A:10, B:6, C:8, D:6
Current counts: A:10, B:7, C:11, D:6
</pre>
</div>
<div id="Ax4.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax4.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax4.SSx1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Action:</span> <span id="Ax4.SSx1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">eliminate at (4,3)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Ax4.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax4.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax4.SSx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Reasoning:</span></p><pre id="Ax4.SSx1.p3.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
Let’s analyze the board and objectives:

**Color targets:** {"A": 10, "B": 6, "C": 8, "D": 6}
**Current counts:** All zero
**Steps left:** 15
**Props:** row (1), col (2), bomb (2), hammer (1)
**Goal:** Maximize score, minimize steps, guarantee level completion.

### Step 1: Find the largest connected group (\geq2) of any color

#### Scan for large groups (to maximize score):

- **Row 0:**
  - "A" at (0,3)-(0,4)-(0,6)-(0,7): 4 connected "A"s (positions [0,3],[0,4],[0,6],[0,7]),
  but not all adjacent.
  - "A" at (0,3)-(0,4): 2 connected
  - "A" at (0,6)-(0,7): 2 connected
- **Row 1:**
  - "B" at (1,2)-(1,3): 2 connected
  - "B" at (1,5)-(1,6): 2 connected
- **Row 3:**
  - "B" at (3,0)-(3,1): 2 connected
- **Row 4:**
  - "B" at (4,3)-(4,4)-(4,5): 3 connected
- **Row 5:**
  - "C" at (5,0)-(5,1): 2 connected
- **Row 6:**
  - "D" at (6,3)-(7,3): vertical, but not connected
  - "B" at (6,1)-(6,2): 2 connected
- **Row 7:**
  - "B" at (7,1)-(7,6): not connected
  - "D" at (7,0)-(7,2): not connected

#### Largest group:
- "B" at (4,3)-(4,4)-(4,5): 3 connected
- "A" at (0,3)-(0,4): 2 connected
- "A" at (0,6)-(0,7): 2 connected
- "B" at (1,2)-(1,3): 2 connected
- "B" at (1,5)-(1,6): 2 connected
- "B" at (3,0)-(3,1): 2 connected
- "C" at (5,0)-(5,1): 2 connected
- "B" at (6,1)-(6,2): 2 connected

### Step 2: Evaluate prop usage

- **Row/Col:** No row or column has a huge concentration of a single color or a large
number of target colors.
- **Bomb:** No 3x3 area contains a large group of a single color.
- **Hammer:** Not efficient for first move.

### Step 3: Best elimination for targets and score

- **Eliminating (4,3) "B" group (3 tiles):**
  - Score: 3*5 + 3*(3-2) = 15 + 3 = 18
  - Progress: 3/6 "B" target
  - Efficient: Yes, largest group, helps with "B" target

### Step 4: Output

**Best action:** Eliminate the 3-"B" group at (4,3)

**JSON:**
{
  "action": {
    "type": "eliminate",
    "pos": [4, 3]
  }
}

**Rationale:**
- Largest available group (3 tiles) for maximum score and target progress.
- Efficient use of step.
- No prop needed yet; save for later if targets become hard to reach.

</pre>
